/** @mainpage Qore ODBC Module

    @tableofcontents

    @section odbcintro Introduction to the ODBC Module

    The odbc module provides an ODBC driver to Qore's DBI system, allowing Qore programs to access many different
    databases using the ODBC interface through the \c Datasource, \c DatasourcePool, and \c SQLStatement classes.

    This module is released under the MIT license (see LICENSE.txt in the source distribution for more information).
    The module is tagged as such in the module's header (meaning it can be loaded unconditionally regardless of how
    the %Qore library was initialized).

    Example of creating an ODBC Datasource using DSN-less connection:
    @code{.py}
# Firebird
Datasource db("odbc:user/pass@{conn=driver=firebird;dbname=localhost:/var/lib/firebird/data/employee.fdb}");

# MySQL
Datasource db = new Datasource(SQL::DSODBC, user, pass, NOTHING, "UTF8", NOTHING,
    NOTHING, {"conn": "driver=MySQL ODBC 5.3 Unicode Driver;Server=192.168.15.1;Database=omquser"});

# PostgreSQL
db = new Datasource(SQL::DSODBC, user, pass, NOTHING, "UTF8", NOTHING,
    NOTHING, {"conn": "driver=PostgreSQL Unicode;Server=192.168.15.1;Database=omquser", "fractional-precision": 6});

# Oracle
db = new Datasource(SQL::DSODBC, user, pass, NOTHING, NOTHING, NOTHING,
    NOTHING, {"conn": "driver=Oracle 12c ODBC driver;Dbq=192.168.15.1:1521/lorem", "bigint-string": True});

# MS-SQL
db = new Datasource(SQL::DSODBC, user, pass, NOTHING, NOTHING, NOTHING,
    NOTHING, {"conn": "driver=ODBC Driver 11 for SQL Server;Server=192.168.15.1;Database=omquser"});
    @endcode

    Example of creating an ODBC Datasource using DSN connection:
    @code{.py}
Datasource db(SQL::DSODBC, user, pass, dsn, "UTF8");
    @endcode

    The ODBC connection string can be given as the \c conn option; if the datasource has a username, it is added as
    both \c USER and \c UID in the connection string, whereas any password is added as both \c PASSWORD and \c PWD in
    the connection string.  To avoid this, use datasource connection parameters without a username or password and add
    the required connection parameters directly to the \c conn option separated by semicolons (\c ";") as in the above
    examples.

    If a database name is provided in the datasource connection arguments, it is added as the \c DSN option in the
    ODBC connection string.

    The driver supports transactions, bound parameters, and prepared statements.
    See @ref odbccapabilitiesguide for supported features and their requirements.

    The driver supports binary parameter bindings and parameter arrays for bulk DML; see @ref odbcbindguide.

    The \c Datasource::getServerVersion() method is implemented for this driver and returns an integer giving the major, minor, and sub version numbers in a single integer according to the following formula:

    @verbatim
major_version * 1000000 + minor_version * 10000 + sub_version
    @endverbatim

    For example: <tt>5030025 => version 5.3.25</tt>

    The \c Datasource::getClientVersion() method is implemented the same way as \c Datasource::getServerVersion() with the same return value format, and returns the version of the specific ODBC driver used for a connection.

    For example: <tt>3010672 => version 3.1.672</tt>

    Like all Qore components, the ODBC DBI driver is thread-safe.

    @section odbc_multiple_results Multiple Results

    SQL can return more than one result: a batch of several statements (for servers and drivers that accept one,
    such as SQL Server and PostgreSQL), a stored procedure that returns row counts and result sets, or DML executed
    with @ref odbcbindguide "parameter arrays" when the driver reports a row count for each parameter set.  The
    driver reads every result:
    - if any statement fails, its error is raised as \c ODBC-EXEC-ERROR with the server's diagnostics, and the
      remaining results are discarded; statements that the server executed before or after it are part of the
      current transaction, so roll it back
    - SQL that returns exactly one result behaves as a single statement always has

    |!Call|!More than one result
    |@ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::exec() "Datasource::exec()", \
        @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::execRaw() "Datasource::execRaw()", \
        @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::select() "Datasource::select()", \
        @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::selectTyped() "Datasource::selectTyped()"|Each result set is returned as a hash \
        of column lists (as a typed result with \c selectTyped()), each row count greater than 0 as an integer; row \
        counts of 0 or less are skipped.  If more than one value is left, they are returned in a hash with keys \
        \c query0, \c query1, ... in order; one value is returned as it is, and no value as 0.
    |@ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::selectRows() "Datasource::selectRows()", \
        @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::selectRowsTyped() "Datasource::selectRowsTyped()", \
        @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::selectRow() "Datasource::selectRow()", \
        @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::selectColumnar() "Datasource::selectColumnar()"|Row counts are skipped and the \
        only result set is returned.  A second result set raises \c ODBC-MULTIPLE-RESULT-SETS-ERROR.  Without a \
        result set, the call behaves as for a single statement that returns none.
    |@ref Qore::SQL::SQLStatement "SQLStatement"|@ref Qore::SQL::SQLStatement::exec() "SQLStatement::exec()" \
        reads the row counts before the first result set, raising the error of a failing statement, and \
        @ref Qore::SQL::SQLStatement::affectedRows() "SQLStatement::affectedRows()" returns the total of all row \
        counts.  The statement returns one result set: when it has been read to the end, the remaining results are \
        read, so the call that reaches the end raises the error of a later failing statement, and a second result \
        set raises \c ODBC-MULTIPLE-RESULT-SETS-ERROR.  The connection can then run other statements while the \
        statement is still open.

    With parameter arrays, the row counts of all parameter sets are added into one count.

    @par Example:
    @code{.py}
# returns {"query0": {"id": (1, 2)}, "query1": 2}
auto rv = db.exec("select id from t order by id; update t set flag = 1 where id < 3");
    @endcode

    Driver notes:
    - SQL Server reports no row counts after <tt>SET NOCOUNT ON</tt>; \c PRINT and \c RAISERROR messages give
      results without a row count, which are skipped
    - FreeTDS runs a batch that is prepared (@ref Qore::SQL::SQLStatement "SQLStatement") or has bound
      parameters as a remote procedure call and reports only the row count of its last statement, and no row
      count before a result set; the Microsoft ODBC Driver for SQL Server reports every row count
    - psqlODBC executes the whole batch when the SQL is executed, and raises the error of any statement then

    @section odbc_cancellation Cancelling Blocked Calls

    A program interrupt (@ref Qore::SandboxManager::requestInterrupt() "SandboxManager::requestInterrupt()") ends
    a call that waits for the server, for example for a lock: the driver cancels the execution, the wait for a
    later result of a batch, or the fetch with \c SQLCancel(), and the call raises \c PROGRAM-INTERRUPTED.  A
    thread cancellation request is raised as \c THREAD-CANCELLED before and after an execution or a wait for a
    later result, and between fetched rows.  The work of a
    cancelled statement is part of the current transaction; roll it back.

